1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Nd4 { The recent correspondence and over the board praxis has established this move to be very promising, yielding Black great results. As we will see, White can easily end up in trouble if he does not proceed cautiously. } 5. Bc4 { In the next chapter we will deal with 5.Ba4 as well as 5.Nxd4 or 5.O-O. } Bc5 6. Nxe5 Qe7 $1 { The key move, not being afraid of any capture on f7. } 7. Nd3 { White hopes to win time by attacking the bishop but Black has a dynamic response at his disposal. -KEY- } d5 $1 { Blasting open the centre. Black gets incredibly quick development. } 8. Bxd5 $2 { After extensive analysis it turns out to be unplayable for White. } Bb6 { Stepping out of Nxc5. I like this ambitious move. Black does not hurry to regain the material, recognising that he can play for more using White's clumsy piece placement. } 9. Nf4 { White is planning to play d3 and complete his development.. We need to act quickly. } c6 { Not only kicking out the bishop with a tempo but also controlling d5 so that White's knight can't jump there. } ( { The immediate } 9... Bg4 { is also good but somehow analysis proves this is even stronger. } ) 
10. Bc4 Bg4 11. Be2 { This is the safest although White is clearly worse anyway. } Nxe2 12. Nfxe2 Nxe4 $17 { Intending ...0-0-0. The powerful bishop pair provides huge compensation for the pawn. } 
